#
# N4 positive control for `broken_manifest_with_an_undescribed_entry_is_rejected`:
# the exact sibling shape of `../undescribed-entry/manifest.ttl`, except this
# manifest's sole `mf:entries` member carries the `rdf:type`/`mf:name`/
# `mf:action` triples the row-grouping SELECT in `crate::manifest::load`
# requires. It must load cleanly, with exactly one case, proving the negative
# fixture's rejection is triggered specifically by the missing description —
# not by some other structural defect (a malformed list, an unresolvable base,
# a parse error) common to both fixtures.
#
# Deliberately placed OUTSIDE `suite/`, for the same reason as its sibling:
# `crates/sparql-conformance/tests/sparql_conformance.rs`'s
# `datatest_stable::harness!` only discovers `suite/**/manifest.ttl`, so this
# file is never picked up as a live conformance case — it exists ONLY to be
# fed to `manifest::load` directly by `crates/sparql-conformance/tests/
# suite_inventory.rs`'s `broken_manifest_with_a_described_entry_loads_cleanly`
# test.
@prefix mf: <http://www.w3.org/2001/sw/DataAccess/tests/test-manifest#> .

<#manifest> a mf:Manifest ;
    mf:entries ( <#describedEntry> ) .

# Unlike <#undescribedEntry> in the sibling fixture, this entry carries all
# three triples the loader's row-grouping SELECT requires to bind a row:
# rdf:type, mf:name, and mf:action (a bare-IRI action, the same shape
# purrdf-extend's own PositiveSyntaxTest/NegativeSyntaxTest cases use — see
# `purrdf_extend_case_count_and_kinds` in `suite_inventory.rs`).
<#describedEntry> a mf:PositiveSyntaxTest ;
    mf:name "describedEntry" ;
    mf:action <describedEntry.rq> .
